  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nAs a low digging down from Lake Winnipeg phases with another low \nlifting from the Central Plains this afternoon, radar imagery shows \nrainfall beginning to move into the far west by Ironwood as of the \ntime of this writing. As the lows become a new, phased low over \nLower MI and Lake Huron tonight, expect the rainfall to continue \nacross the rest of the U.P. the rest of this afternoon through this \nevening, with generally light rainfall seen across the entire area \nover the next 12 hours. Within the synoptic phase of this system, \nexpect a wetting rainfall across the area as guidance shows \ngenerally around half an inch of liquid falling. However, as robust \nCAA cycles down behind the phased low late tonight through Friday, \nexpect lake enhanced rainfall to pick up over the north wind belts, \neventually transitioning over to pure lake effect rain showers by \nFriday as the low pulls away and delta-Ts in the mid to upper teens \nkeep the lake effect going until high pressure ridging returns \nFriday night through this weekend. Additional rainfall amounts from \nthe lake enhanced and effect rain showers late tonight through \nFriday could bring an additional half inch or more of liquid to the \nnorth wind belts, with some of the higher elevations in the upslope \nareas potentially seeing a 15 to 40% chance of seeing an inch and a \nhalf of liquid between now and early Friday afternoon (according to \nthe latest REFS run). With high pressure ridging returning for the \nweekend, expect the last of the lake effect rain showers to wrap up \nby Friday night. One last note on the lake effect showers: there \ncould be a transition over to snowfall as the lake effect showers \nwrap up Friday night. While accumulations should practically be \nnothing, it is worth noting that these could be the first snowflakes \nof the season. \n\nWith precipitation looking to stay away from the area this weekend \nthrough the first half of next week, the WAA seen with the high \npressure ridging moving overhead could induce elevated fire weather \nconditions across the area this weekend, particularly over western \nUpper MI. This is due to very dry air coming in above the boundary \nlayer (think 925mb level); with mostly sunny skies overhead, robust \nmixing during the daytime hours could bring RHs down into the 20 \npercents (lowest on Saturday) and southwesterly wind gusts of 30 to \n40 mph to the sfc (strongest on Sunday). In addition, poor RH \nrecoveries are expected over the western U.P. Saturday night (<80% \nMaxRH). While the recent rainfall will help alleviate drought \nconcerns, with plenty of dead leaves being added to the sfc and the \ndrought conditions not expected to be completely removed after the \nrainfall this afternoon through Friday, we could see some critical \nfire weather concerns this weekend across the area. Therefore, as \nreminder, you should obey all burn bans in place if you are under \none (which can be found at www.dnr.state.mi.us/burnpermits). In \naddition, avoid power equipment that creates sparks, and for \nsmokers, please properly discard your cigarettes, cigars, etc. While \nthe fire weather danger decreases once we enter the first half of \nthe work week next week due to calmer winds, we could see some \nfairly dry sfc conditions still on Monday and Tuesday (MinRHs down \nto 30%). Rainfall may finally return by the latter half of next \nweek.",
